  "name": "Question 12",
  "problem": "$\\frac{\\sqrt{45}}{\\sqrt{2}}=$",
  "worked_solution": "To remove the radical $\\sqrt{2}$ from the denominator, all we need to do is multiply both the numerator and denominator by $\\sqrt{2}$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{45}}{\\sqrt{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{45}}{\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{45}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{45}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we notice that $45$ has a perfect square factor $(9).$ Therefore, we can rewrite $\\sqrt{45}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{45} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{9\\cdot 5} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{9}\\cdot \\sqrt{5} & = \\\\ 3\\sqrt{5} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify our original fraction as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{45}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}}{2} & = \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}}{2} & = \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{5\\cdot 2}}{2} & = \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
